  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nNorthwest flow aloft with an axis of surface high pressure from the\nLower MS Valley thru the Ohio Valley. This resulted in mainly clear \nskies and light winds overnight. Temperatures dipped down to lows in\nthe the upper 20s to the lower 30s. A hard freeze was not observed. \nHighs expected to reach the upper 50s this afternoon under abundant \nsunshine. \n\nMid level short wave drops thru the eastern Great Lakes today into\ntonight. A weak associated trof/front slides into the region\ntonight and falls apart. Ahead of this front only expect some high \nlevel clouds and a tightening of the pressure gradient with southwest\nwinds gusting up to 25 to 30 mph this afternoon with highest gusts\nacross the northern counties. \n\nOnly expect a few scattered clouds associated with the front as\nsurface high pressure noses into the southwest late tonight. \nAlthough temperatures will not be quite as cold as this morning - lows\nin the mid to upper 30s will lead to the formation of frost. \nTherefore, have a frost advisory for tonight thru 9 AM Monday \nmorning. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nMid level flow will back more westerly to start the week with \nridging at the surface and aloft offering dry conditions and above\nnormal temperatures. Highs are expected in the mid 50s to around 60 \nMonday, the lower and middle 60s Tuesday, and then 60 to 65 Wednesday.\n\nA digging system will result in the passage of a surface cold front \nlate Wednesday into Thursday. This system leads to a mid level low \nthat develops over the Great Lakes late in the week and continues to\ninfluence our weather into the first part of the weekend.  \n\nThis will lead to a chance of rain beginning Wednesday night and \ncontinuing into Thursday. The mid level low will keep the threat for \nprecipitation in the forecast into Friday and perhaps Saturday.  \n\nTemperatures will turn colder with highs in the 50s Thursday, the \n40s for Friday and Saturday.",
